\title{RiemannFinMath: bond and OIS curve calculations from a Rust library}
\description{
The calculations are in Rust. The R functions convert dates and return tibbles. There is no
global state such as an evaluation date: dates, calendars and conventions are arguments.
}
\details{
Results are compared with QuantLib, not with market data. Agreement with QuantLib is not a
check against actual market holidays or market conventions, and QuantLib's defaults are not
market conventions. Conventions therefore have no defaults here.

Names of choices are snake_case strings:
\describe{
\item{Day counts}{\code{actual_360}, \code{actual_365_fixed}, \code{act_act_isda}, \code{act_act_isma},
\code{thirty_360_bond_basis} and others; \verb{business_252:<calendar>} for Business/252.}
\item{Compounding}{\code{simple}, \code{compounded}, \code{continuous}.}
\item{Business day conventions}{\code{following}, \code{modified_following}, \code{preceding},
\code{modified_preceding}, \code{unadjusted}, \code{half_month_modified_following}, \code{nearest}.}
\item{Date generation rules}{\code{backward}, \code{forward}, \code{zero}, \code{third_wednesday},
\code{third_wednesday_inclusive}, \code{twentieth}, \code{twentieth_imm}, \code{old_cds}, \code{cds}.}
\item{Calendars}{See \code{\link[=is_business_day]{is_business_day()}}.}
\item{Overnight indices}{\code{tonar}, \code{estr}, \code{sofr}, \code{sonia}.}
}

Dates are \code{Date} values or ISO strings (\code{YYYY-MM-DD}). Functions with \code{on_error} stop at the
first row that fails with \code{"stop"}; with \code{"na"} they keep \code{NA} for that row.

A defect in the Rust code panics: its file and line are printed on stderr, and R receives an
error and keeps running.
}
